::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q01::A question asks which portfolio piece best shows growth. Which answer is strongest?{
~A. The piece the writer likes most but cannot explain.#Incorrect. Preference alone does not show growth.
~B. The piece with no revision history.#Incorrect. Growth is easier to show when revision evidence is present.
=C. The piece revised from feedback with a clear craft improvement.#Correct. This provides evidence of growth and author control.
~D. The piece with the most pages only.#Incorrect. Length alone does not prove growth.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q02::A final file has a missing title and visible draft comments. What skill is being tested?{
=A. Publication readiness.#Correct. Title, draft comments, and final file presentation are publication-readiness issues.
~B. Character motivation only.#Incorrect. The issue is final presentation, not character development.
~C. Meter only.#Incorrect. The question is not focused on poetry rhythm.
~D. Research citation only.#Incorrect. This unit focuses on portfolio and publication decisions.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q03::Which answer best shows craft reasoning?{
~A. The story is about moving away.#Incorrect. This is topic summary.
=B. I used clipped dialogue so the reader feels the tension between speakers.#Correct. This names a craft choice and effect.
~C. I worked hard.#Incorrect. Effort is not craft reasoning.
~D. It is my favorite scene.#Incorrect. Preference does not explain craft.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q04::Which presentation plan is strongest?{
~A. Read the excerpt with no title or focus.#Incorrect. The audience needs orientation.
~B. Summarize the whole portfolio without reading anything.#Incorrect. The task requires focused presentation of creative work.
~C. Apologize for the draft and stop.#Incorrect. This does not present author craft.
=D. Introduce title and genre, give brief context, read a focused excerpt, and explain effect.#Correct. This follows the author presentation process.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q05::Which answer should you avoid on a synthesis assessment?{
~A. The answer with specific evidence and reader effect.#Incorrect. That is usually a strong answer.
~B. The answer that connects revision to craft growth.#Incorrect. That supports synthesis.
=C. The vague answer that says "make it better" without a specific action.#Correct. Vague answers do not show mastery.
~D. The answer that identifies publication formatting problems.#Incorrect. That is useful when the question is about readiness.
}
